{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>On the eighth day you shall have a solemn assembly (atzeret).</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the word עצרת means that you are restrained (עצורים) from doing labor. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that Shemini Atzeret is a festival unto itself with respect to פז\"ר קש\"ב (a mnemonic for its distinct laws): its פרוסה (meal-offering) is distinct, its זמן (time) is distinct, its רגל (festival) is distinct, its קרבן (sacrifice) is distinct, its שיר (song) is distinct, its ברכה (blessing) is distinct — that is, the blessing and prayer in which one mentions this eighth day, the festival of atzeret; and one does not sit in the sukkah on it; and with respect to שהחיינו; and with respect to mourning — for one who buries his dead on the eve of the festival, the decree of thirty days given by the Sages to prohibit haircutting is lifted from him because of this eighth day. For they said: one day before the festival counts as seven days of mourning, and the festival is seven, and its eighth day counts as seven — here are twenty-one days; and he counts nine more and is then permitted to cut his hair. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Sukkah: it is written here ״פרים״ (bulls, plural) and it is written here ״פר״ (bull, singular); it is written here ״וביום״ and it is written here ״ביום״. The explanation of the matter is that on the other days they would offer bulls each and every day, but on the eighth day one bull; and on the other days it says ״וביום״ (and on the day), but on the eighth day it says ״ביום״ (on the day) without the vav, to tell you that it is a festival unto itself." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> On the eighth day you shall have an atzeret — 'I have restrained (עצרתי) you before Me,' like a king who invited his sons to a banquet for so many days; when the time arrived, he said: 'I beg of you, stay with me one more day, for your departure is hard for me.' And in the Gemara of Sukkah, chapter HaHallel, Abaye said: the seventy bulls correspond to the seventy nations; the single bull — why? Correspond to the single nation. It is like a mortal king who said to his servants, 'Make me a banquet'; at the end he said to his beloved, 'Make me a small banquet so that I may enjoy you.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> This atzeret is Knesset Israel, for there everything is gathered (נעצר), and it is an expression of kingship, as in (I Samuel 9:17) ״זה יעצור בעמי״, and likewise (Judges 18:7) ״יורש עצר״. And therefore the eighth day is a festival unto itself, and it is the etrog, which stands by itself and is not bound together with the lulav; and it is the festival of the day of the giving of the Torah, which the Sages of blessed memory called Atzeret — it is the very expression that the Torah itself used for this eighth day, and on that very day we heard His words from within the fire. But the seventh day of Pesach, which is part of the festival, Scripture called atzeret, and it is the Yesod; and each one is called by the name atzeret just as each one is called by the name Shabbat, for Knesset Israel is the mate of Shabbat. And concerning the Yesod, David of blessed memory would compose praise and said (Psalms 92:1) ״מזמור שיר ליום השבת״, and concerning Knesset Israel he would compose praise (ibid. 10) ״למנצח על השמינית״. And set it upon your heart: the expression ״עצרת תהיה לכם״ is like the expression (Leviticus 11:45) ״להיות לכם לאלהים״. And in the section 'Emor el ha-kohanim' (Leviticus 23) it is written ״עצרת היא״, and the meaning of עצרת is 'of her,' for the word is in construct form, and it is as if it said 'the atzeret of Shabbat,' which is called ״אות היא ביני וביניכם״. And set it upon your heart that therefore the masters of truth called this eighth day סעודה קטנה (a small banquet), and it is the small א of ״ויקרא״ (Leviticus 1), and it is the small ה of ״בהבראם״ (Genesis 2), and it is the small י of ״תשי״ (Deuteronomy 32), and it is the secret of what they said: 'nine and a small one combine' — that is, for prayer and for zimun; for prayer according to the one who says so, and for zimun, which is the sustenance of His world, according to all. And it is the secret of what they further said (Psalms 37:25) ״נער הייתי גם זקנתי״ — this verse the prince of the world spoke. And so Isaiah of blessed memory mentioned it concerning the First Temple and said (Isaiah 28:10) ״זעיר שם זעיר שם״ — all these are one matter and one path for them."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The mnemonic פז\"ר קש\"ב expands to six categories listed after it; resolved each abbreviation from the list that follows in the text.", 2 "The phrase 'שהוא שהוא' appears to contain a duplicated שהוא; rendered as written without smoothing. Citations expanded to chapter:verse (e.g., 'ibid. 10' for Psalms 6 which he gives as שם י) following the normalize rule."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:07.055072703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}